WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:01.570
- I just spit.

00:00:01.570 --> 00:00:03.660
- I hope my grandma never watches this.

00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:04.780
- What's up? I'm Joelle.

00:00:04.780 --> 00:00:05.613
- I'm Danielle.

00:00:05.613 --> 00:00:06.790
- I'm Micah and I have.

00:00:06.790 --> 00:00:07.623
- Never made.

00:00:07.623 --> 00:00:08.456
- Kimchi before.

00:00:08.456 --> 00:00:10.540
- My family is half Chinese, half Korean.

00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:11.930
And growing up, both cultures

00:00:11.930 --> 00:00:15.380
definitely had a heavy
impact on our upbringing.

00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:17.430
However, I never took the time to learn

00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:19.130
how to make some really fundamental

00:00:19.130 --> 00:00:20.660
Korean and Chinese dishes.

00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:23.060
And kimchi is undoubtedly one

00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:25.520
of the pinnacles of Korean cuisines.

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:27.050
I decided to challenge my sisters

00:00:27.050 --> 00:00:28.770
on this wonderful learning experience.

00:00:28.770 --> 00:00:30.630
Each of us will be making our own kimchi

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:33.073
without a recipe, for the first time.

00:00:33.073 --> 00:00:35.540
And after we let it stew and ferment

00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:37.930
my parents are going to
do a blind taste test

00:00:37.930 --> 00:00:42.080
and see which of our kimchis
is the most accurate.

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:44.270
- To be honest, I'm not
much of a cook in general.

00:00:44.270 --> 00:00:48.150
I would say that I'm a big
frozen dumplings kind of person.

00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:50.120
- In general, in the kitchen
I'm very comfortable.

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:51.550
I love to cook for my family.

00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:53.940
- I am not comfortable in the kitchen.

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:55.533
Well, okay, I can make pasta.

00:00:58.570 --> 00:00:59.950
So I know there is cabbage.

00:00:59.950 --> 00:01:02.160
- Which I salted for all of us.

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:02.993
- Gochujang.

00:01:02.993 --> 00:01:05.440
- Red pepper flakes
which is the gochugaru.

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:07.910
- There are those baby shrimp thingies.

00:01:07.910 --> 00:01:09.530
- Sugar.

00:01:09.530 --> 00:01:10.880
- I think there's garlic.

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:12.017
- Fish sauce.

00:01:12.017 --> 00:01:13.930
- (laughs) Is that it?

00:01:13.930 --> 00:01:16.503
- Calling on the
ancestors to come through.

00:01:18.130 --> 00:01:20.790
- I like how there are like
these two measuring spoons.

00:01:20.790 --> 00:01:21.802
As if I have any idea,

00:01:21.802 --> 00:01:25.650
like how much of anything
should go in there?

00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:27.460
- I don't know, this is like a tablespoon.

00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:29.610
- All I know about the
kimchi preparation process

00:01:29.610 --> 00:01:32.300
is that it's effectively
a paste that you make.

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:33.950
And then you just rub all over the kimchi.

00:01:33.950 --> 00:01:35.320
You let it sit for like a week.

00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:37.990
- I know that it's usually
done in like big batches,

00:01:37.990 --> 00:01:39.325
like I think normally.

00:01:39.325 --> 00:01:41.310
And in like Korean dramas

00:01:41.310 --> 00:01:43.830
you see them doing it like
pounds and pounds of cabbage.

00:01:43.830 --> 00:01:45.300
Even my grandma when she would do it

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:48.259
I think she did it like
for a huge batch at a time.

00:01:48.259 --> 00:01:49.599
- Oh my gosh.

00:01:49.599 --> 00:01:50.890
- (Danielle dry heaves)

00:01:50.890 --> 00:01:53.280
A serving size is one tablespoon.

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:55.940
So I'm going to assume that I
should use a whole tablespoon.

00:01:55.940 --> 00:01:57.770
- Being able to make
traditional Korean dishes

00:01:57.770 --> 00:01:58.920
is really important to me because

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:01.920
I feel like growing up
in a Asian-American home,

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:03.450
second slash third generation.

00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:07.260
- It's definitely harder to
connect with my cultural roots.

00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:08.695
- It's because growing up in America

00:02:08.695 --> 00:02:10.990
our parents are taught to assimilate.

00:02:10.990 --> 00:02:15.210
So it wasn't necessarily
part of growing up

00:02:15.210 --> 00:02:17.270
to do some really key things

00:02:17.270 --> 00:02:18.530
that I think were probably more normal.

00:02:18.530 --> 00:02:20.700
- Back in my parents' day

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:22.483
Is a teaspoon too much sugar?

00:02:23.570 --> 00:02:24.403
Oh well.

00:02:24.403 --> 00:02:26.170
I would say that this
is probably something

00:02:26.170 --> 00:02:28.033
I feel guilty not knowing how to do.

00:02:28.930 --> 00:02:30.370
And that's it.

00:02:30.370 --> 00:02:33.560
- So I'm mixing my three things together.

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:36.100
'Cause yeah, I don't know
what else goes in it.

00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:37.470
- It's too thick.

00:02:37.470 --> 00:02:40.390
